# Utility Function Conventions

This document defines coding standards for utility functions in the `lib/` directory.

## File References
@lib/jsonUtils.ts
@lib/urlUtils.ts
@lib/clipboardUtils.ts
@lib/types.ts

## Core Principles

### 1. Pure Functions
Utility functions should be pure (no side effects, same input = same output):

```typescript
// Good: Pure function
export function validateJson(jsonString: string): { isValid: boolean; error: string | null } {
    if (!jsonString.trim()) {
        return { isValid: false, error: 'JSON input is empty' };
    }
    try {
        JSON.parse(jsonString);
        return { isValid: true, error: null };
    } catch (error) {
        return { isValid: false, error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : 'Invalid JSON' };
    }
}

// Bad: Side effects
export function validateJson(jsonString: string): boolean {
    console.log('Validating:', jsonString);  // Side effect
    globalErrorState = null;  // Mutation
    // ...
}
```

### 2. Single Responsibility
Each function should do one thing well:

```typescript
// Good: Single responsibility
export function getJsonType(value: unknown): JsonValueType { ... }
export function isLeafNode(value: unknown): boolean { ... }
export function buildPath(parentPath: string, key: string, isArrayIndex: boolean): string { ... }

// Bad: Multiple responsibilities
export function processJson(value: unknown): { type: string; isLeaf: boolean; path: string } { ... }
```

